      "sectionBreaks": [
        {
          "before": "Circulation means adding the tangential contributions of a field along a closed path.\nThese laws link time dependence with spatial variation; they do not require both fields to be nonzero at every point, or describe one field disappearing before creating the other.",
          "after": "A description with only an electric field or only a magnetic field must specify the reference frame.\nIn an electrostatic model with a stationary charge and no other magnetic fields, the charge produces only an electric field (Figure 101)."
        },
        {
          "before": "The original textbook says that any frame moving relative to the magnet can reveal an electric field; more precisely, this depends on the local magnetic field and the direction of relative motion.\nThese examples do not imply that every electromagnetic field can be made purely electric or purely magnetic by changing frames.",
          "after": "Electric and magnetic fields are two kinds of components of the same complete **electromagnetic field** in a given reference frame.\nThe original textbook calls the electromagnetic field a special form of matter and emphasizes that its objective existence does not depend on human knowledge.\nThis is the textbook's philosophical description, not an additional experimental law."
        },
        {
          "before": "Modern physics describes measurable electromagnetic processes through forces on charges and the transfer of energy and momentum.\nChanging reference frames changes the electric and magnetic description; it does not mean that an observer's thoughts create the field.",
          "after": "The electric and magnetic components of the same electromagnetic field can change with the reference frame.\n**Physical laws have the same form in all inertial reference frames; no inertial frame is privileged because of its state of motion.**"
        }
      ],
